find the second-largest distinct element in an array. This solution runs in 
  >> (O(n)\) time complexity and uses \(O(1)\) extra space by traversing the array exactly once.

public class SecondLargest {
    public static Integer findSecondLargest(int[] arr) {
        if (arr == null || arr.length < 2) {
            return null; // Not enough elements to have a second largest
        }

        int largest = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        int secondLargest = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        boolean foundDistinct = false;

        for (int num : arr) {
            if (num > largest) {
                secondLargest = largest;
                largest = num;
            } else if (num < largest && num > secondLargest) {
                secondLargest = num;
                foundDistinct = true;
            }
        }

        // Handle cases where all elements are the same or no valid second distinct element exists
        if (secondLargest == Integer.MIN_VALUE && !foundDistinct) {
            return null; 
        }

        return secondLargest;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr1 = {12, 35, 1, 10, 34, 1};
        int[] arr2 = {10, 10, 10}; // Duplicate elements case

        System.out.println("Second largest in arr1: " + findSecondLargest(arr1)); // Output: 34
        System.out.println("Second largest in arr2: " + findSecondLargest(arr2)); // Output: null
    }
}

>> Single Pass Strategy: The algorithm tracks the largest and secondLargest variables simultaneously while looping through the array.

>> Distinct Values Filtering: By checking num < largest && num > secondLargest, 
    it ignores duplicate instances of the largest number, ensuring the result is strictly the second-largest distinct element.
>> Edge Case Safety: It properly returns null if the array has fewer than two unique numbers (e.g., [10, 10, 10]).

